{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 8, :comments ["<b>Take the staff.</b> There are many interpretations here; there is the view of an individual, because he said to Israel, 'Hear now, you rebels' — and they are children of Abraham, Isaac, and Jacob — but if so, why did he further say to them, 'You have continued to rebel'? And others said that the word ודברתם [and you shall speak] is like והכיתם [and you shall strike], and like it ותדבר את כל זרע המלוכה [and she destroyed all the royal seed], but this is not correct, for the word is from the root דבר, or from ידבר עמים תחתנו [He subdues peoples under us]; and behold, ותדבר means like ותאבד [and she destroyed], and likewise in the second book. And behold, the meaning of ודברתם אל הסלע is 'and you shall destroy the rock.' Furthermore, if this is the interpretation, why was Moses punished? They also answered: because he struck twice; but if so, why was Aaron punished? And Rabbi Moses ha-Darshan, the Spaniard, of blessed memory, said: there are signs performed through speech and there are through action; and speech is like the word of Elisha; and HaShem commanded him to take the staff to strike the rock, like the manner of the Rock [at Horeb]; and He added the word ודברתם to bring out the water through striking and through speech; and because Israel provoked him, he said to them, 'Shall we bring forth water for you from this rock?' and his intent was that we have no power to bring water from the rock except by the power of HaShem; but he did not articulate his speech well, and some people thought in their hearts that his speech meant that HaShem could not bring water from the rock, and this is the meaning of 'you did not sanctify Me'; and he brought proof from the words of the Psalmist, who said, 'because they rebelled against his spirit and he spoke rashly with his lips' — so the sin was in his speaking, not in the striking. This too is not correct, for Moses said this, so why was Aaron punished? And furthermore, there is no mention of speech in the account, and the proof he brought is no proof, for the meaning of 'because they rebelled against his spirit' is only concerning HaShem; and so it is written, 'But they rebelled and grieved His holy spirit'; and this is the meaning of 'and they embittered [the waters] of Meribah' — that they embittered HaShem; and likewise it is written, 'These are the waters of Meribah, where the children of Israel quarreled with YHWH, and He was aggrieved with Moses on their account, because they rebelled against the spirit of HaShem,' and He decreed upon Moses that he not enter the land. And others said that the speech to the rock, which does not hear, is nothing but [accomplished] through striking, and if so, why was Moses punished? And others said: because they did not recite a song, as with 'Then Israel sang this song' — and this is the meaning of 'you did not sanctify Me.' And others said that HaShem commanded them to speak, and they did not speak; rather Moses struck; therefore they were both punished; and it is said to them: 'You trespassed, you rebelled, you did not believe, you did not sanctify.' And men of balanced judgment said that it is impossible that the emissary of HaShem would alter the word of HaShem, and if he altered, how would we believe in his Torah? These too did not speak correctly, for had he exchanged a commandment, he would have been punished immediately — but he was punished for something that is not a commandment for all time and not a statute for Israel, and was not done intentionally but in error, because Israel provoked him; and so he said, 'Also with me HaShem was angry because of you.' And others said that Israel said he should bring water from a different rock, as in 'and you shall see that rock' — and Moses feared to alter the word of HaShem; and because he did not believe to bring water from the rock that they specified, therefore he was punished; and this is the meaning of 'you did not believe in Me.' This interpretation too is not correct, for Scripture says 'you rebelled against My word,' and they did not rebel. And the correct interpretation in my eyes I shall reveal in hints. Know that when the part knows the whole, it cleaves to the whole and effects through the whole signs and wonders. And the truth is that HaShem said to Moses and to Aaron, 'and you shall speak' — and they did not speak, because of the people's quarrel with Moses; and the part acted as a part, and struck the rock, and water did not come out until he struck it a second time; and they did not sanctify HaShem, and rebelled and trespassed in error. Also the interpreter may interpret 'and he spoke rashly with his lips' concerning Moses, for therefore he was punished, because it was not fitting for him to speak anything until he had fulfilled the mission of HaShem; and it is also the derash of our ancestors of blessed memory, that the sin was because he said, 'Hear now, you rebels'; and they hinted at this sod:" "<b>To the rock.</b> That which I have informed you [of], or [it means] the one found near the camp:" "<b>And it shall give its waters.</b> Because of 'its waters' many erred and said that this rock is the Rock [of Horeb], and these are blind of heart, for the Rock was at Horeb and this rock was at the edge of the land of Edom, for it was at Kadesh. It is also possible to interpret 'its waters' as the waters that I shall place in it:"], :book :numbers, :voice :ibn-ezra, :lang :en, :chapter 20, :notes {0 "Ibn Ezra quotes several partial biblical phrases without identifying them; the references are left implicit as in the original. 'Then Israel sang' is Exodus 15:1; 'and you shall see that rock' is not a direct quotation but apparently an interpretive paraphrase or otherwise unclear; left untranslated as such."}, :source-witnesses [["On Your Way" "http://mobile.tora.ws/"]], :timestamp "2026-07-09T19:18:48.727305539Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}